smoltcp lends its sockets their buffers for as long as they live, and
has no way to take a socket or a buffer apart again: SocketSet::remove
gives the socket back, but not the memory it was given. The kernel
lends each socket memory from its heap (ADR 0180), which must come back
when a program closes the socket (ADR 0182). This adds `into_buffers`
to the TCP, UDP and ICMP sockets and `into_storage` to RingBuffer and
PacketBuffer, which give the socket or buffer up and return what it was
made with.

SocketSet's `add`, `get`, `get_mut` and `remove` panic when the set is
full or a handle is not the set's; the kernel must not panic because a
program closed a socket twice or opened too many. This adds `try_add`,
`try_get`, `try_get_mut` and `try_remove`, which return an `Option`
instead (and `try_add` the socket that did not fit).

SocketSet goes through every slot of its storage each time it iterates
its sockets, several times on every poll, though the kernel gives each
interface 128 slots and few hold a socket. It now keeps where its last
socket is and stops there.

A FIN that comes in FIN-WAIT-1 or FIN-WAIT-2 while the delayed-ACK timer
runs, for data that came before it or with it, is acknowledged only when
the timer ends, 10 ms by default: the other end waits in LAST-ACK that
long, and the socket owes the ACK in TIME-WAIT. Linux acknowledges such a
FIN at once (tcp_fin), and so does this; the kernel takes a loopback
connection in TIME-WAIT apart at once (ADR 0185), which must not lose
that ACK.

A TCP connection whose two ends are sockets of the same interface, as
on the loopback interface, can pass its octets straight into the other
end's receive buffer, with no segment between them, as Solaris's TCP
fusion does (ADR 0186): the kernel copies them there from the sending
program, as it would have copied them into the transmit buffer. This
adds the calls each end makes for it, which count what passes as a
segment sent, received and acknowledged at once, and a window that a
read opens as told by an ACK: `next_across`, `room_across`,
`receive_across`, `sent_across`, `window_across`, `told_window_across`
and `heard_window_across`. The kernel pairs the sockets, and passes
octets only when no segment between them is on its way.

To be offered upstream.

